  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure ridging will shift eastward throughout the day\ntoday, giving way to meager height falls aloft as a weak trough\nand shortwave advance towards the eastern Great Lakes. While\nthese features won't quite rotate into our region, they will be\nthe driving forcing mechanisms behind scattered showers and \nthunderstorms beginning Thursday and again on Friday. Overall, \nthe forcing associated with this system is very weak. However, \nour position relative to a strengthening Bermuda High will \nensure warm, moist air gets ushered into the region and force \nrequisite destabilization to support convective development. \n\nThe challenge with Thursday's forecast, particularly pertaining\nto the potential for isolated severe storms, lies with the \nextent of cloud cover. A weak surface disturbance will pass by \nto our south tonight, driving some initial showers and clouds \nearly tomorrow morning. With moisture advection marrying up to \nthe height falls with the incoming trough, it is very possible \nthat we could have sufficient coverage to limit severe- level \nstorm growth. That said, given the high level of heat and \nhumidity (see KEY MESSAGE 2 for additional details), latest CAMs\nindicate moderate instability with weak to modest deep layer \nshear particularly within the Capital District and areas south \nwhere SPC has introduced a Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 5) for \nsevere weather. Isolated strong to severe storms are therefore \npossible with strong to locally damaging winds the primary \nhazard due to precipitation loading and dynamic cooling (lapse \nrates ~6 C/km). Any shower or storm will also therefore be \ncapable of producing moderate to heavy downpours given \nunseasonably high PWATs (~2 STDEVs above normal) up to 2\" and \ntall, skinny CAPE profiles. We will have to monitor especially \nour urban areas for potential isolated flash flooding given very\nwet antecedent conditions after last week's event. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe aforementioned Bermuda High building to our southeast will\nbe responsible for driving heat and humidity back to\nuncomfortable levels on Thursday and Friday. Geopotential\nheights reaching ~2 STDEVs above normal along with anomalous \nmoisture content courtesy of persistent southwesterly flow will \ndrive highs widely into the mid to upper 80s with dewpoints in \nthe upper 60s to low 70s both days. Cloud cover remains a\nquestion on Thursday so the magnitude of heat and humidity\ntherefore is also somewhat uncertain. However, confidence is\nhigh that the greatest feels-like temperatures will be located\nin Valley areas both days where heat index values will reach the\nlow to potentially mid/upper 90s. Heat Advisories may be needed\nin the near future particularly for portions of the Mid-Hudson\nValley.",
